WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.856 --> 00:00:03.491
A large backlog can make
everything feel urgent.

2
00:00:04.227 --> 00:00:08.013
The analyst starts by separating
historical priority from active work.

3
00:00:08.365 --> 00:00:11.128
Of thirteen hundred and
sixty high-priority issues,

4
00:00:11.651 --> 00:00:14.115
twenty are marked in
progress in this snapshot.

5
00:00:22.220 --> 00:00:25.092
The follow-up asks what those
twenty actually represent.

6
00:00:25.505 --> 00:00:28.012
They include bugs, tasks, and stories.

7
00:00:28.662 --> 00:00:32.449
Product areas with large historical
totals can have no active records,

8
00:00:32.716 --> 00:00:35.588
so count alone is not a useful
ranking of business risk.

9
00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:45.976
Open a Checkout issue to inspect the source.

10
00:00:46.451 --> 00:00:48.531
The title, date, priority,

11
00:00:48.755 --> 00:00:51.944
and status are visible, along
with the other matching records.

12
00:00:52.595 --> 00:00:55.514
The analyst keeps the distinction
between selecting a record for

13
00:00:55.539 --> 00:00:57.619
review and proving its business impact.

14
00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:08.452
The final handoff asks product
to confirm affected customers,

15
00:01:08.804 --> 00:01:11.353
link incidents, and compare
impact with effort.

16
00:01:11.938 --> 00:01:14.914
Approved fixes can then be
assessed against a baseline.

17
00:01:15.352 --> 00:01:17.773
The tool turns a broad prioritization question

18
00:01:17.912 --> 00:01:20.674
into a reviewable queue
and a practical next step.
